-- title:
--   Toric divisor class from two Pl-points with common reduction
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $M \ge 5$ is an integer not divisible by $p$, $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which by `hK` equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Further, $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$, and $j \in K$ is an element whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function (`hj`), with $j \ne 0$. Write $X =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) and $f =$ [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) $: X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ for the two-chart integral model glued from the finite and the infinite chart algebras, and assume $f$ proper (a typeclass hypothesis, together with the smoothness `hsm` and separatedness `hsep` of `D.toBase` recorded below).
--
--   Special fibre data. $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra; $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change $X \times_A k$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ (elements of `SchemeHomOver`, so each commutes with the structure maps); `hcover` says every point of $X \times_A k$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced, `hn` names its cardinality $n$ and `hn0` requires $n > 0$.
--
--   Sections. $\varepsilon$ is a section of $f$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ are $k$-sections of $c_1$, $c_2$, and `hε₁` says that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   Relative Picard data. $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for $f$: a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism `D.toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section. `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents, via a rigidified Poincaré bundle on $X \times_A D.P$, the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor of $(f,\varepsilon)$ cut out by `algEquivZeroCut`, i.e. by the condition `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero` on rigidified line bundles, with the universal property that every rigidified bundle satisfying the condition is, up to isomorphism, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along a unique morphism to `D.toBase`, and with the Poincaré bundle trivial along the zero section. `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for the base change $X \times_A k$ with the base-changed section and the base-changed designation `D.baseChange k`, and `hPk` asserts that its Poincaré bundle is isomorphic to the bundle obtained from the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some`, pulled back along the first projection of $D.P \times_A k$ and transported by `BaseChange.ofR`. Similarly $D_1$, $D_2$ are relative Pic$^0$ designations for $c_1$, $c_2$, represented by `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` with respect to $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ and the same cut. The morphism $\nu_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ goes from the base of `D.baseChange k` to the base of $D_2$, and `hν₂` states that for every $k$-scheme $T$ and every $T$-point $a$ of `(D.baseChange k).toBase`, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification (in the sense of `Scheme.Modules.rigidify` along the rigidifying section of $c_2$) of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$; thus $\nu_2$ is the restriction-to-$C_2$ homomorphism.
--
--   Geometric model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ is an $A$- and $L$-algebra compatibly; $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), that is, a proper smooth integral curve together with an isomorphism of its function field with that intermediate field and a bijection between its closed points and the places; $e_\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ to $X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `heη` saying it is compatible with the structure morphisms. The instance `Mη_chart_nonempty` says the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin` says that for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra `chartAlgFin A K j` the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` obtained from the germ of $a$ at the generic point through $M_\eta$'s function-field identification has Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffMap (algebraMap L (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) applied to the expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for every $g \in \mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ fixing $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$, if $x'$ followed by the chart morphism equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ and the chart morphism, then the place attached to $x'$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under the action of [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54).
--
--   Special-fibre group datum. $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups `G.J0s`, `G.JI`, `G.JE`, a subgroup `G.torus` of `G.J0s`, and a surjective homomorphism `G.proj : G.J0s →+ G.JI × G.JE` whose kernel is `G.torus`. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify `G.J0s`, `G.JI`, `G.JE` with the $k$-points of the bases of `D.baseChange k`, $D_1$, $D_2$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that in each case the pullback of the relevant Poincaré bundle along the point attached to a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks along the two summands; and `hproj` says that for every $x$ the $D_1$-point attached to the first component of `G.proj x` is `pts x` followed by the pullback homomorphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some`, while the $D_2$-point attached to the second component is `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   Igusa identification of the components. $w$ is an [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16) (a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction over $k$ is nonzero), and $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$, $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $k$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), together with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ over $k$ (`he₁`, `he₂`).
--
--   Generic dictionary and Abel–Jacobi data. `gpts` is a bijection from [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the group $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of degree-zero divisor classes of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`, onto the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of `D.toBase`, additive for the relative group law of `hrep.some` (`hgadd`). Over $L$: `hDL` is the representability statement for $X \times_A L$ with the base-changed section and `D.baseChange L`; `ajL` is a morphism over that curve to the base of `D.baseChange L`; `kL` is a morphism $X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to X \times_A L$ with `hkL₁`, `hkL₂` expressing compatibility with both projections; `ajbar : Mη.C ⟶ D.P` is defined by `hajbar` as $e_\eta$ followed by $k_L$, by `ajL` and by the first projection, and `hajbar_over` records that it lies over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M_\eta.C$ with `hεbar` saying it reduces to $\varepsilon$ in the chart and `hεbar_aj` saying it is carried by `ajbar` to the zero section. `hPL` compares the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` with the transport of that of `hrep.some`, `hajLε` says that the section composed with `ajL` is the zero section of `D.baseChange L`, and `hajL` is the Abel–Jacobi property: for every field $K'$, every $\operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every point $x$ of $X \times_A L$ above it, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` at $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor at the base-changed section. Finally `hpts_aj` says that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$ in the chart there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` equal to the difference of the one-point divisors at the places of $x$ and of $s$, with `gpts (Pic0.mk Dv)` given by $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   The place and its reduction. $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $Pl$ (`hPl`), $\rho : A \to Pl$ is a ring homomorphism compatible with $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`), and $\pi_k : Pl \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism (`hπk`) with $\pi_k \circ \rho$ the structure map $A \to k$ (`hAlgk`).
--
--   The two points. $P$ and $P'$ are $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $M_\eta.C$ and $Dv$ is a degree-zero divisor of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` which by `hDv` equals $(P') - (P)$, i.e. `Finsupp.single (Mη.pointEquivPlace P') 1 - Finsupp.single (Mη.pointEquivPlace P) 1`. The morphisms $Pt, Pt'$ are $Pl$-points of $X$ above $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$ with `hPt`, `hPt'` saying that $P$, respectively $P'$, followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection, factors as $\operatorname{Spec}(Pl \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $Pt$, respectively $Pt'$. The morphism $u_\kappa : \operatorname{Spec} k \to X \times_A k$ satisfies `huκ` (it is a section of the projection to $\operatorname{Spec} k$) and `huκ₁`, `huκ₂`: its first projection equals the reduction along $\pi_k$ of $Pt$ and also of $Pt'$, so the two $Pl$-points have the common reduction $u_\kappa$. The hypothesis `hsame` requires that the image of the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ under $u_\kappa$ lies in $\operatorname{range} i_1 \setminus \operatorname{range} i_2$, or else in $\operatorname{range} i_2 \setminus \operatorname{range} i_1$; each of the two disjuncts is stated as the conjunction of the relevant condition with itself.
--
--   The candidate class. $s$ is a $Pl$-point of `D.toBase` above $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$ such that `hgen`: the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along $s$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` at $Pt'$ tensored with the ideal module of `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` at $Pt$; and `hsp`: for every $y$ in `G.J0s` whose associated $k$-point `pts y` composed with the first projection equals the reduction $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k)$ followed by $s$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along `pts y` is isomorphic to the line bundle of `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` at $u_\kappa$ tensored with the ideal module of the same divisor.
--
--   Conclusion. There exist a $Pl$-point $z$ of `D.toBase` above $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$ and an element $y$ of `G.J0s` such that: first, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `gpts (Pic0.mk Dv)` attached to the class of $Dv$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(Pl \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $z$; second, the $k$-point `pts y` followed by the first projection of $D.P \times_A k$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k)$ followed by $z$; and third, `G.proj y = 0`, i.e. $y$ lies in `G.torus`.
--
--   This is the step in the study of $J_1(Mp)$ at a place above $p$ which shows that the class of $(P') - (P)$ is toric when the two points admit $Pl$-integral extensions with a common reduction lying on exactly one of the two Igusa components of the special fibre: the $\operatorname{Pic}^0$-point classifying $\mathcal{O}(Pt') \otimes \mathcal{O}(Pt)^{-1}$ matches the generic dictionary value at $[(P')-(P)]$ and reduces to a point of the special fibre group killed by the projection to the component Jacobians, hence lying in the torus. It is used in the deduction of the corresponding statement for classes coming from inertia at $p$.
